## The idea

Animals are nodes; a conflict is an edge. We need to split them into two groups so that no edge stays inside a group — that is checking whether the graph is **bipartite**. Colour one animal "left", all its conflicts "right", their conflicts "left", and so on (breadth-first). If we ever need to give an animal the same colour as a neighbour, it is impossible (there is a cycle of odd length). The input may list a conflict in one direction only, so first make every edge two-way.

## Step by step

1. Build a two-way adjacency list from the conflicts. An animal in conflict with itself → impossible.
2. Go through the animals in increasing id order. If one has no colour yet, colour it 0 (left) and BFS from it.
3. In the BFS, give every uncoloured neighbour the opposite colour; if a neighbour already has the SAME colour → false.
4. Split: run the same colouring and return the two groups, each sorted. Starting from the smallest id puts it on the left.

## Worked example

**Input:** {1: [2], 3: [1, 2]}

| Step | What happens |
|---|---|
| edges | 1–2, 3–1, 3–2 (made two-way) |
| animal 1 | colour left; neighbours 2 and 3 → right |
| animal 2 (right) | neighbour 3 is also right → conflict inside one group |
| result | a triangle cannot be split in two |

**Result:** false

## Cost

O(V + E) time and memory.

## The code (C#, compiled and tested against every part of the question)

```csharp
public static class AnimalGrouping {

    public static bool CanSplit(Dictionary<int, List<int>> conflicts) {
        Dictionary<int, int> side = Colour(conflicts);
        return side != null;
    }

    // Left gets the smallest animal id; both lists sorted.
    public static (List<int> Left, List<int> Right) Split(Dictionary<int, List<int>> conflicts) {
        Dictionary<int, int> side = Colour(conflicts);
        if (side == null) throw new InvalidOperationException("The animals cannot be split.");
        List<int> left = new List<int>();
        List<int> right = new List<int>();
        foreach (KeyValuePair<int, int> pair in side) {
            if (pair.Value == 0) left.Add(pair.Key);
            else right.Add(pair.Key);
        }
        left.Sort();
        right.Sort();
        return (left, right);
    }

    // Returns animal -> side (0 or 1), or null if no valid split exists.
    private static Dictionary<int, int> Colour(Dictionary<int, List<int>> conflicts) {
        Dictionary<int, int> side = new Dictionary<int, int>();
        if (conflicts == null) return side;

        // 1. Two-way adjacency list (the input may list a conflict in one direction only).
        Dictionary<int, List<int>> graph = new Dictionary<int, List<int>>();
        foreach (KeyValuePair<int, List<int>> pair in conflicts) {
            if (!graph.ContainsKey(pair.Key)) graph[pair.Key] = new List<int>();
            if (pair.Value == null) continue;
            foreach (int other in pair.Value) {
                if (other == pair.Key) return null;              // conflicts with itself
                if (!graph.ContainsKey(other)) graph[other] = new List<int>();
                graph[pair.Key].Add(other);
                graph[other].Add(pair.Key);
            }
        }

        // 2. Colour each connected group, starting from the smallest ids.
        List<int> animals = new List<int>(graph.Keys);
        animals.Sort();
        foreach (int start in animals) {
            if (side.ContainsKey(start)) continue;               // already coloured
            side[start] = 0;
            Queue<int> queue = new Queue<int>();
            queue.Enqueue(start);
            while (queue.Count > 0) {
                int current = queue.Dequeue();
                foreach (int next in graph[current]) {
                    if (!side.ContainsKey(next)) {
                        side[next] = 1 - side[current];          // opposite group
                        queue.Enqueue(next);
                    } else if (side[next] == side[current]) {
                        return null;                             // same group as an enemy
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        return side;
    }
}
```

## Likely follow-up questions

**Q: Self-conflict? An animal with no conflicts? A one-way declaration?**

Self-conflict is unsplittable; isolated animals go anywhere; build the relation symmetric

**Q: Complexity?**

O(V + E) time and space

**Q: Many threads query the same map.**

Read-only input → no locks; do not mutate it

**Q: Ten million animals.**

Iterative BFS, not recursion; adjacency as arrays of ints

**Q: A seating service calls this per request.**

Precompute components; cache by version of the conflict list
